    "passage": [
      "A wave is a disturbance that transfers energy from place to place without carrying matter along with it. Mechanical waves, such as sound and water waves, need a medium to travel through; electromagnetic waves, such as light, do not. In a transverse wave the medium oscillates at right angles to the direction of travel, as on a shaken rope. In a longitudinal wave it oscillates along the direction of travel, as in sound, which is a series of compressions and rarefactions.",
      "Every periodic wave has a wavelength, the distance between successive crests, a frequency, the number of waves passing a point each second, measured in hertz, and an amplitude, the size of the oscillation. The period is the time for one wave, T = 1/f. Wave speed equals frequency times wavelength, v = fλ. The speed is set by the medium, not by the frequency: if the frequency rises in the same medium, the wavelength gets shorter.",
      "Sound is a longitudinal mechanical wave, so it cannot travel through a vacuum. It moves at about 343 m/s in air at 20 °C, and faster in water and in solids. The pitch we hear depends on frequency, and humans hear roughly 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz. Loudness depends mainly on amplitude, which is related to the energy the wave carries.",
      "When waves meet, their displacements add, a principle called superposition. Crest meeting crest gives constructive interference and a larger wave; crest meeting trough gives destructive interference, which noise-cancelling headphones use. The Doppler effect is the change in observed frequency when the source and observer move relative to each other: an approaching siren sounds higher in pitch and a receding one lower, although the siren itself emits the same frequency throughout."
