  {
    "title": "Electric Circuits",
    "category": "physical-sciences",
    "subject": "Physics",
    "topic": "Electricity",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about current, voltage, resistance, Ohm's law, series and parallel circuits, and electrical power. Answer in your own words, explain why and not only what, and say you don't know when you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Electric current is the rate at which charge flows, I = Q/t, measured in amperes. By convention current flows from the positive terminal to the negative one around the outside of a circuit, although in metal wires the moving charges are electrons, which drift the other way. Voltage, or potential difference, is the energy transferred per unit of charge, measured in volts, where one volt is one joule per coulomb. A battery provides a potential difference; it does not supply electrons that get used up, because charge is conserved all the way around the circuit.",
      "Resistance opposes current and is measured in ohms. Ohm's law states that the current through a conductor is proportional to the voltage across it, V = IR, provided the temperature and other conditions stay constant; conductors that obey it are called ohmic. A wire's resistance depends on its material, and increases with its length and decreases with its cross-sectional area. For most metals it also rises with temperature.",
      "In a series circuit the components form a single loop, so the same current flows through each, the voltages across them add up to the supply voltage, and the total resistance is the sum, R = R1 + R2. A break anywhere stops the whole circuit. In a parallel circuit each branch has the full supply voltage, the branch currents add up to the total current, and 1/R = 1/R1 + 1/R2, so the total resistance is less than that of the smallest branch. Household wiring is parallel, so each appliance works on its own.",
      "Electrical power is the rate of energy transfer, P = IV, which with Ohm's law also equals I²R and V²/R. Energy equals power times time. Electricity companies bill energy in kilowatt-hours: a 1,000-watt heater running for one hour uses one kilowatt-hour, which is 3.6 million joules."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "current-voltage", "title": "Explain current and voltage", "description": "Defines current and potential difference and explains what a battery supplies.",
