        "facts": ["Current is charge per unit time, measured in amperes.", "Voltage is energy per unit charge, measured in volts.", "Charge is conserved around a circuit; a battery provides energy, not new charge."],
        "misconceptions": ["Current is used up as it goes around a circuit.", "A battery stores a fixed amount of electrons that it gives out."] },
      { "key": "ohms-law", "title": "Apply Ohm's law", "description": "Uses V = IR and names what a wire's resistance depends on.",
        "concept": "Resistance and Ohm's law", "definition": "For an ohmic conductor at constant temperature, current is proportional to voltage, V = IR.",
        "facts": ["Voltage equals current times resistance for an ohmic conductor.", "Resistance increases with wire length and decreases with cross-sectional area.", "A metal's resistance usually rises with temperature."],
        "misconceptions": ["Ohm's law holds for every component, including lamps and diodes.", "A thicker wire has more resistance."] },
      { "key": "series-parallel", "title": "Compare series and parallel circuits", "description": "Explains how current, voltage and total resistance behave in each arrangement.",
