    "passage": [
      "In a chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged into new substances. Antoine Lavoisier established the law of conservation of mass: in a closed system the total mass of the products equals the total mass of the reactants, because atoms are neither created nor destroyed. A chemical equation must therefore be balanced, with the same number of each kind of atom on both sides, as in 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Equations are balanced by changing coefficients, never subscripts, since changing a subscript changes the substance.",
      "Chemists count particles in moles. One mole is 6.022 × 10²³ particles, Avogadro's number. The molar mass of a substance, in grams per mole, has the same numerical value as the mass of one of its particles in atomic mass units: water has a molar mass of about 18.02 g/mol, so 18.02 grams of water contain one mole of molecules.",
      "The coefficients of a balanced equation give the ratios in which substances react, in moles, not in grams. In 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O, two moles of hydrogen react with one mole of oxygen to give two moles of water. To find a mass of product, convert the given mass to moles, use the mole ratio, then convert back to grams.",
      "When reactants are not present in the exact ratio, the one that runs out first is the limiting reactant: it fixes the most product that can form, the theoretical yield, while the others are left in excess. The amount actually obtained is usually less, because of side reactions, incomplete reactions and losses during handling. Percent yield is the actual yield divided by the theoretical yield, times 100."
