  {
    "title": "Acids, Bases and pH",
    "category": "physical-sciences",
    "subject": "Chemistry",
    "topic": "Acids and Bases",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about definitions of acids and bases, the pH scale, strong and weak acids, and neutralization and buffers. Answer in your own words, explain why and not only what, and say you don't know when you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Svante Arrhenius defined an acid as a substance that produces hydrogen ions, H⁺, in water, and a base as one that produces hydroxide ions, OH⁻. In water the hydrogen ion actually joins a water molecule to form the hydronium ion, H₃O⁺. The broader Brønsted-Lowry definition calls an acid a proton donor and a base a proton acceptor. When an acid gives up a proton, what remains is its conjugate base; ammonia, NH₃, is a base because it accepts a proton from water, even though it contains no hydroxide.",
      "The pH scale measures acidity: pH = −log[H⁺], where [H⁺] is the hydrogen ion concentration in moles per litre. At 25 °C pure water has a pH of 7, which is neutral; lower values are acidic and higher values basic. Because the scale is logarithmic, each step of one pH unit is a tenfold change, so a solution at pH 3 has a hundred times the hydrogen ion concentration of one at pH 5.",
      "A strong acid, such as hydrochloric acid, ionizes completely in water. A weak acid, such as acetic acid in vinegar, ionizes only partly, so most of its molecules stay intact. Strength is not the same as concentration: a dilute solution of a strong acid can have a higher pH than a concentrated solution of a weak one.",
      "In neutralization, an acid and a base react to form a salt and water, as in HCl + NaOH → NaCl + H₂O. A titration finds an unknown concentration by adding a solution of known concentration until an indicator changes colour at the end point. A buffer, a mixture of a weak acid and its conjugate base, resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added; buffers in blood keep its pH close to 7.4."
    ],
